    'install.linux.chatTitle': 'Open the chat and add your model',
    'install.linux.chatOpen': 'On this machine, open <code>http://localhost:3000</code>. From a laptop, tunnel to it first:',
    'install.linux.chatModel': 'Enter the <strong>chat access token</strong>. Then go to <strong>Settings → Server</strong>, unlock it with the <strong>admin token</strong>, add your model server (<code>{address}</code>), click <strong>List models</strong>, choose one and save. Give it a task.',
    'install.linux.chatModelKey': 'Enter the <strong>chat access token</strong>. Then go to <strong>Settings → Server</strong>, unlock it with the <strong>admin token</strong>, add your model server (<code>{address}</code> with your API key), click <strong>List models</strong>, choose one and save. Give it a task.',
    'install.linux.chatModelGpu': 'Enter the <strong>chat access token</strong>. Then go to <strong>Settings → Server</strong>, unlock it with the <strong>admin token</strong>, add your model server (<code>{address}</code>, or your GPU box\'s address), click <strong>List models</strong>, choose one and save. Give it a task.',
    'install.linux.updatesTitle': 'Turn on one-click updates',
    'install.linux.updates': 'When a new release is out, the chat shows a banner. With Cedar\'s small updater set up, its <strong>Update</strong> button (with the admin token) downloads the release, checks it against the release\'s checksums, installs it, restarts both services, and goes back to the old version if Cedar doesn\'t come up.',
    'install.linux.updatesNote': 'Run it from the folder you unpacked Cedar in. <code>sudo cedar-update</code> does the same from a shell. More in the <a href="{url}">Linux guide</a>.',
    'models.q.where.label': 'Where should the model run?',
